---
import type { TocItem } from '../consts';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

// 本文は仮執筆 v0.1。英語化時にすべて差し替える。
const toc: TocItem[] = [
  { id: 'asymmetry', label: 'The asymmetry of generation and verification' },
  { id: 'bottleneck', label: 'When approval becomes the bottleneck' },
  { id: 'engineering', label: 'Treating responsibility as an object of engineering' },
  { id: 'science-first', label: 'Why start with scientific research' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Thesis"
  description="The institutions of verification and approval, designed for an era when generation was scarce, have not kept pace with the speed of AI generation."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Thesis</p>
    <h1>Approval becomes responsibility only when it is connected to evidence</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The institutions of verification and approval, designed for an era when generation was scarce, have not kept pace with the speed of AI generation.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section id="asymmetry" class="section">
    <h2>The asymmetry of generation and verification</h2>
    <p>
      AI now generates intellectual work — analyses, figures, prose, code — far beyond the pace at which humans can produce it. The institutions of verification and approval, designed for an era when generation was scarce, have not kept up with this change. When the volume of output exceeds human capacity to verify it, the rate-limiting step shifts from generation to approval.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bottleneck" class="section">
    <h2>When approval becomes the bottleneck</h2>
    <p>
      When approval becomes the bottleneck, it turns into a formality. People sign off without being able to verify the evidence, and their signatures lose the power to establish where responsibility lies when something goes wrong. Accountability comes not from a signature but from a connection to verifiable evidence. Approval that is not connected to evidence is not a transfer of responsibility but a disappearance of it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="engineering" class="section">
    <h2>Treating responsibility as an object of engineering</h2>
    <p>
      We treat responsibility not as a virtue entrusted to individual attention and ethics, but as something that can be engineered. Just as software engineering moved "correctness" from human attention into mechanism — through type checking, continuous integration, and audit logs — the outputs AI generates need a layer that mechanically maintains the consistency between evidence and outputs, and that third parties can verify independently.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="science-first" class="section">
    <h2>Why start with scientific research</h2>
    <p>
      Licklider starts building this layer in scientific research, because science is the domain where responsibility (authorship) and verifiability (reproducibility) are demanded most explicitly as institutions, and where AI generation is spreading fastest. Engineering accountability that holds in science becomes the prototype for every domain that involves regulation, auditing, and consequential decisions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="page-foot">
    <p class="version">Thesis v0.1 — 2026-08-16</p>
    <p class="next">
      Next section: <span class="placeholder" title="Coming soon">What We Build →</span>
    </p>
  </footer>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .page-head .eyebrow {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .standfirst {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .section {
    padding: 2.75rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint);
  }

  .section h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .section p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .page-foot {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding: 2rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }

  .page-foot p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
